            ":question/id": "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a",
            ":question/math-academy-id": "e-181",
            ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$",
            ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3})^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
          },
          ":knowledge-point/id": "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee",
          ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186096842,
          ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
            ":db/id": 17592186096401,
            ":question/id": "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb",
            ":question/math-academy-id": "e-182",
            ":question/problem": "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$",
            ":question/worked-solution": "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
          },
          ":knowledge-point/id": "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204",
          ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186096843,
          ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
            ":db/id": 17592186096402,
            ":question/id": "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b",
            ":question/math-academy-id": "e-183",
            ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$",
            ":question/worked-solution": "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2})^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{\u2032}(x) & =(1-3x)^{\u2032}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={f}^{\u2032}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
          },
          ":knowledge-point/id": "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3",
          ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"
        }
